# RecoveryPact rules

RecoveryPact is a governed database-recovery system: two NVIDIA Nemotron guardians
negotiate, a deterministic authority decides what may run, a restricted executor acts
and a verifier checks the business recovered. Business context is federated through
Zetaris; inference and memory go through Meterless (Nightshift, H-MEM).

## Safety, never negotiable
- Agreement is not permission. Never add a code path where model output executes an
  action without the authority gate (`backend/app/authority/gate.py`).
- Tools you build for Cursor are read-only or replay-only. No live incidents, no
  executor calls, no model-provider calls.
- Never read, print, copy or commit `.env` or any token. Never put a key in code,
  docs, tests or commit messages.

## Results are evidence
- Never edit anything under `evals/results/`, `recordings/`, `logs/`,
  `output_examples/` or `demo/data/`. They are published benchmark artifacts.
- A number you write in docs must come from `summary.json` or the validator
  (`python -m app.evals.validate <dir>`), not from memory.

## Git
- Other work in this repo may be uncommitted and is not yours. Stage only the files
  you created or changed, by explicit path (`git add tools/recoverypact-mcp docs/cursor.md ...`).
  Never `git add -A`, `git add .`, `git commit -a`, `git stash`, `git reset` or
  `git checkout -- <file>` on files you did not write.
- Run `git status` before committing and check `.env` is not staged.
- Small conventional commits (`feat(mcp): ...`, `docs(cursor): ...`, `test(mcp): ...`).

## Style
- Python 3.10+, standard library first, type hints, short docstrings that say why.
- Match the surrounding code: comment density, naming, no speculative abstractions.
